WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
In about ninety seconds, we’ll build a
local billing package

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.860
under a blueprint you approve.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.860
Watch the package explicitly check access,
the blueprint

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.540
set the rule, and your application supply
the trusted customer

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:14.180
ID.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.340
Northwind’s lookup succeeds.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.178
Change the customer, and it’s denied.

00:00:20.582 --> 00:00:24.102
Create the billing package and add its
charge lookup. Before

00:00:24.262 --> 00:00:27.622
reading the local ledger, the package
checks the named operation

00:00:27.702 --> 00:00:31.622
and the requested customer ID. Check the
package, then publish

00:00:31.702 --> 00:00:32.742
it to the local store.

00:00:34.562 --> 00:00:37.762
Define, review and approve the blueprint
yourself, with an

00:00:37.882 --> 00:00:41.842
agent's help if useful. Require customer
ID from the application,

00:00:42.362 --> 00:00:45.282
deny by default, and allow this lookup
only when its

00:00:45.362 --> 00:00:48.882
argument matches that context. Then lint
the blueprint.

00:00:50.626 --> 00:00:54.306
Start the server with a fresh local token.
The application

00:00:54.386 --> 00:00:57.746
selects the configured blueprint, binds
the customer ID for this

00:00:58.186 --> 00:01:01.666
request, and sends the program with that
token. The response

00:01:01.946 --> 00:01:03.746
reports two charges and their total.

00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:09.353
Now run the second program. It keeps the
useful total,

00:01:09.673 --> 00:01:13.433
then tries the same lookup for another
customer. That customer

00:01:13.593 --> 00:01:16.953
ID fails the blueprint rule, so the
runtime denies the

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:20.393
lookup and returns an error instead of a
result. The

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:24.513
application supplies request context; the
runtime enforces the

00:01:24.553 --> 00:01:25.513
configured check.
